  "text": "Solution 2 is the most likely to be correct, though I could not verify it against the source paper.\n\n- **Solution 1 (7):** The rotation argument only shows that some lower bound holds. The claim that a 3-regular construction gives exactly 7 longest paths is asserted, not shown, so it does not establish the value.\n- **Solution 3 (6):** The construction is vague (two blocks at a cut vertex, \"three Hamiltonian paths\" each), and the claim that 6 is a sharp lower bound for all n ≥ 18 is unsupported.\n- **Solution 2 (4):** It cites a known result and a specific 18-vertex extremal example, which matches the threshold n ≥ 18 in the problem. My recollection is that the research literature on this minimum-longest-paths problem gives a(3,n) = 4 for n ≥ 18, though I cannot confirm that from memory alone. Its \"at least 4 longest paths sharing a common vertex\" lower-bound statement is also not something I can verify here.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
